; expect 42
; Two structs that share field NAMES at different OFFSETS, read and
; written through `.field` - the six lines the QA sweep of 2026-08-18
; said would have caught the by-name resolution from the day it was
; inherited from stage0.
;
; Until 2026-08-21 `q.tag` resolved by name across the whole struct
; registry, first declaring struct winning: `P` declares `tag` at word
; 0, so `q.tag` read `Q`'s word 0 - the String header `q.name` - and
; `(set q.tag 7)` stored 7 over it. `check` said OK, `build`
; succeeded, and the program died of SIGSEGV in `strLen`. The checker
; now stamps every resolved field access with the receiver's struct
; (`stampFieldStruct`) and the emitter resolves through the stamp
; (`resolveStructField`), so each of the four accesses below lands in
; its own struct's slot. Both declaration orders are covered by the
; one pair: `P` is first and right either way, `Q` is the one the old
; rule got wrong.
;
; 30 + 7 + 5 = 42. Under the old rule the exit status was a pointer's
; low byte at best and a signal at worst.
(import Str)

(pub struct P
  (tag : Int)
  (name : String))

(pub struct Q
  (name : String)
  (tag : Int))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (p (P 0 "zz"))
    (q (Q "defgh" 0))
  )
    {
      (set p.tag 30)
      (set p.name "abc")
      (set q.tag 7)
      (+ (+ p.tag q.tag) (strLen q.name))
    }
  )
)
